# Supabase. Required from Phase 1 onward.
#
# Build-time trap: the static menu pages read Supabase during `next build`, so
# the NEXT_PUBLIC_* pair must exist in the Vercel *Preview* environment scope as
# well as Production, or preview builds fail. The menu readers still fall back
# to a static catalog so an env-less build cannot produce an empty store.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

# Bypasses row level security entirely, so it belongs here and in the Vercel
# environment and nowhere else. Reached only through lib/supabase/admin-client.ts.
#
# Its first real consumer is the address rate limit on checkout: rate_limit_hit
# is granted to service_role alone (0010), so this is what lets the Server Action
# call it. Leaving it unset does not break ordering, because the limiter fails
# open by specification, but it does mean that control is silently off. Set it.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# Canonical origin. Leave unset locally; siteUrl() falls back to localhost.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=http://localhost:3000

# The single account allowed to bootstrap or retain Super Admin access.
SUPER_ADMIN_EMAIL=you@example.com

# Web Push. Generate with: npx web-push generate-vapid-keys --json
#
# The public key is 87 characters. A shorter value (for example a pk_-style
# key pasted by mistake) makes the opt-in button vanish with no error anywhere
# and zero rows in push_subscriptions. Assert the length at startup.
#
# GENERATE ONCE PER ENVIRONMENT, THEN KEEP IT. A browser's subscription is
# bound to the public key that created it, so replacing this pair does not
# rotate a credential, it orphans every registered device: each one keeps a
# subscription the server can no longer send to, silently, until somebody taps
# the opt-in again. Treat it as an identity, not a password.
#
# Use a DIFFERENT pair for local development than for production, so a laptop
# can never send an alert to a real counter tablet.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ means the public key is inlined when the site is BUILT, not read
# when a request arrives. Setting it on the host and not redeploying changes
# nothing. See docs/push-device-test-checklist.md for how to tell.
NEXT_PUBLIC_VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=
VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=
VAPID_SUBJECT=mailto:you@example.com

# ZenPOS. Leave unset until the discovery checklist in the implementation
# prompt (section 16.2) has been answered. Until then the manual re-key adapter
# is the live POS path.
ZENPOS_BASE_URL=
ZENPOS_API_KEY=

# PayMongo online prepay. Optional, and dark until app_settings enables it.
#
# ALL THREE, OR NONE. Two of them make a payment possible and the third makes it
# confirmable, so a deployment holding fewer than three declines to offer online
# payment at all (lib/paymongo/config.ts). The missing webhook secret is the one
# worth naming: with it absent the customer pays, the signed paid event is
# rejected as forged, the counter never sees the order, and the expiry sweep
# cancels it while the money sits with PayMongo. No error appears anywhere.
#
# A KEY IS ITS MODE, and the prefix is the only place that is written. PayMongo
# accepts each half of a mismatched pair on its own, so a test secret beside a
# live public key is two working credentials for two different accounts, and the
# intent is created where the payment will never arrive. Startup says so.
#
# The webhook signing secret is returned ONCE, when the endpoint is created.
# `node scripts/paymongo.mjs webhook:create <url>` registers the four events
# this app reconciles and writes the secret here.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_PAYMONGO_PUBLIC_KEY is read at BUILD time by next.config.ts (the
# QR image host) and by the Content Security Policy. Setting it on the host
# without redeploying leaves the QR code blocked.
PAYMONGO_SECRET_KEY=
NEXT_PUBLIC_PAYMONGO_PUBLIC_KEY=
PAYMONGO_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# Local development only. This replaces PayMongo API calls with an explicit
# success/failure simulator. It is ignored in production. Set the existing
# app_settings PayMongo flag and QR Ph method on in the development database.
#
# IT WINS OVER REAL KEYS. startPayment checks this before it reaches PayMongo,
# so leaving it true simulates every payment no matter how correct the keys
# are. Set it false to exercise a real test-mode rail.
MOCK_PAYMENTS_ENABLED=false

# Transactional email. Optional, and dark until app_settings.email_enabled is
# true as well. Both switches must be on: these say sending is POSSIBLE, the
# database flag says it is WANTED, and the flag can be flipped without a deploy.
#
# Its one consumer today is the franchise lead alert
# (lib/email/franchise-alert.ts). No key means no mail and a form that behaves
# exactly as it did before, which is why this can ship unset.
#
# RESEND_FROM MUST USE A DOMAIN VERIFIED WITH THE PROVIDER. That is not a
# preference, it is how mail providers stop spam, and an unverified domain is
# refused at send time rather than at deploy time. Without a domain, Resend's
# own onboarding sender works but delivers ONLY to the address the Resend
# account was created with, which is a real interim answer rather than a broken
# one: the alert still reaches a human the moment a lead arrives.
RESEND_API_KEY=
RESEND_FROM=NY Buffalo Brad's <alerts@your-verified-domain>

# Where a franchise lead alert is sent. Defaults to franchise@5bdf.ph, the
# address the site already publishes. Set it to the Resend account's own address
# while no domain is verified, since that is the only place the onboarding
# sender can deliver.
FRANCHISE_ALERT_TO=

# Guards the cron endpoints. Compared in constant time.
CRON_SECRET=set-a-long-random-string
